WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.590
SPEAKER: Last week
we established

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:05.610
that CORS is more misunderstood
than full-on Monster.

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.720
And today we're
diving a bit deeper.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:10.350
We'll be learning about
two types of CORS request

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:11.550
and configuration.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.980
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:18.730
As you might remember,
we left our heroes

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.740
as they narrowly
avoided a CORS error.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:23.440
And now they must
learn to differentiate

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.050
between the different
types of CORS requests.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:28.360
There are two types
of CORS request--

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.520
simple and preflighted.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.290
A simple request
can be initiated

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:34.750
directly-- that simple.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:39.070
A preflighted request must send
a preliminary preflight request

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:42.250
to the server to get permission
before the primary request can

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:43.300
proceed.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.330
That is, the client has
to ask and the server

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:49.870
has to give the green light
before the data can be served.

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:51.880
There are several
circumstances under which

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:53.680
request is preflighted.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.070
It uses methods other
than GET, HEAD, or POST.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:59.950
It uses the POST method
with a content type

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:01.780
other than these types.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:03.770
Or it sets custom headers.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:06.160
For example, if
your app uses PUT

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:09.130
to ensure idempotent
requests, this request

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.020
would need to be preflighted.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.300
Let's look at what happens
when a browser makes

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.910
a simple request
to cloud storage.

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:19.000
The browser adds origin
header to the request.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.430
The origin header contains,
as you might have guessed,

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:24.760
the origin of the
resource seeking to share

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.950
the other domain's resources.

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:30.850
Cloud storage compares the
HTTP method of the request,

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:34.510
like GET or POST, and the
value of the origin header

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:37.420
to the methods and origins
information in the target

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.120
cloud storage buckets
CORS configuration

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:42.670
to see if there are matches.

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:45.580
If there are, cloud
storage origin includes

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:49.910
the Access-Control-Allow-Origin
header in its response.

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:53.260
The Access-Control-Allow-Origin
header contains the value

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:56.390
of the origin header
from the initial request.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:59.770
The browser then receives the
response and checks to see

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:02.080
if the
Access-Control-Allow-Origin

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:05.710
value matches the domain
specified in the original

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:06.910
request.

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:10.000
If they do match,
the request succeeds.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.510
If they don't match or if the
Access-Control-Allow-Origin

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:18.470
header is not present in the
response, the request fails.

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:21.560
A preflighted request
performs some additional steps

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:25.630
first before following the
same steps in a simple request.

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:29.470
First, the browser sends an
OPTIONS request containing

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.330
the requested
method and requested

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:33.820
headers of the primary request.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:38.050
The server responds back with
the values of the HTTP methods

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:41.140
and headers allowed by
the targeted resource.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:44.530
If any of the method or header
values in the preflight request

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.810
aren't in the set of
methods and headers allowed

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:49.840
by the targeted resource,
the request fails

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.780
and the primary
request isn't sent.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:56.170
If the method or header
values are included,

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:59.530
the request continues like
a simple CORS request.

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:03.850
Once again, this is a true
byte of the full CORS meal.

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:06.100
So make sure you check
out the documentation

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:07.870
to learn some more.

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:10.540
But let's talk about
how you configure

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:13.390
CORS for Cloud Storage.

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:16.330
Cloud storage allows you
to set CORS configuration

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:18.633
at the bucket level only.

00:03:18.633 --> 00:03:20.050
You can set the
CORS configuration

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:25.420
for a bucket using the gsutil
command line tool, the XML-API,

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:27.480
or the JSON-API.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:32.020
Let's look at setting up CORS
configuration with gsutil.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.900
First, I need a
CORS JSON document.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.980
Here's an example.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:40.720
This JSON document explicitly
allows cross-origin GET

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:44.580
and POST requests from
origin1.example.com,

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.520
but not HEAD.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:50.170
It may include the
Content-Type response header.

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:52.210
Then I set the CORS
policy of the bucket

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:54.340
to the JSON document.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:57.040
To remove the CORS
configuration of a bucket,

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.630
the gsutil command is the same.

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:03.505
The difference is that the
CORS JSON document is empty.

00:04:03.505 --> 00:04:06.430
Now you know a bit more
about CORS, as well as

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:08.860
how to set the CORS
configuration for a cloud

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:10.580
storage bucket.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:13.750
If you want to know even more
about cloud storage and CORS,

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:15.940
check out the links
in the description.

